 I think we do it well and I  think that you know Martha Gellhorn did  it till she was well into her 90s I  don't want to be into my 90's doing it  but I think that you know we we have  there is something we do that's quite  Noble  Janene and I are making it sound like  it's all misery actually you know you  have fun too and that's what Kim really  described so well that you wouldn't keep  going to these places if all it was was  relentless misery a week ago I was at  the most wonderful Kurdish wedding on  the border between Turkey and Syria all  those people there had fled Syria had  terrible stories didn't know when they  would see their home again but they had  fun that night they really made a lot of  effort to enjoy the wedding to show  their children that Kurdish dancing and  music you know you could forget just for  a few hours the misery of what was  happening to their home just you know  less than an hour's drive away and I  think one of the wid is things that ever  happen to me which brought this home to  me after Benazir was killed in Pakistan  she was a good friend of mine and I  thought how is Pakistan gonna survive  this and there was kind of riots in her  home province of Sindh and people talks  about Pakistan breaking up and all of  this sort of apocalyptic stuff and I  arrived at the airport and got taxi  driver and journalist notoriously always  asked the taxi driver what's going on  and they and he said oh it's very grim  everything's very bad so I said yeah you  know Venice is death and everything and  he said to me no we have no discos and I  said what and he said to me we don't  have any discos in Pakistan you know I  was fourteen years of foreign  correspondent and it's so true what they  say it's all about high highs and low  lows and when you come back after all  these high highs you kind of forget the  low lows and and for you guys who and  even myself we've we've lost colleagues  and and gotten killed I mean the lows  are low but the highs are super high and  then at some point you come back and  you're kind of kind of happy to to just  do the middle but it's never like it is  when you're abroad right  they notice that every answer to every  question  [Applause]  you to really exemplify that camaraderie  and and that sisters-in-arms  mentality that I think exists when one  works overseas because there are still  very few of us women in the field doing  this kind of work and so I think it is  very important that we stick up for each  other and that we that there is  solidarity between us and I was a bit  surprised by Jeanine saying at the  beginning that she didn't find that  because I have always found other women  and I think you know the most upsetting  thing as a woman correspondent as a  mother is when other women attack you  for what you do because it isn't easy I  [Music]  am now because I wasn't mentored because  there weren't older women that supported  me and my you know in now I have interns  all the time I have young women I try to  to make them you know I would never say  to someone all the time young people say  to me is it worth becoming a journalist  I think yes absolutely it's the greatest  job in the world so I do I do think  there's solidarity absolutely watch the  movie you know and friends of mine  before the movie was coming out they're  saying hey is that is that Tania  Vanderpool Marc Marc Margot Robbie's  character is that based on me and I said  no because you're not that pretty and  also you're nice you know and because  it's like we just had this group of  people and some of them are in the  audience actually who all just helped  each other and you know we would always  make sure that everybody was taken care  of you talk about stories I just felt no  sort I mean I would have done anything  and I'd still do anything to help the  you know female reporters I worked with  over there and what's nice is like when  you have that you're sort of in this  you're sort of you can call it the kebab  well you can call it whatever but you're  in the sort of zone where you will be  friends for life and you know we didn't  even we barely met each other over there  but like I think that we've like we've  got all the similar sort of she was  saying answers them I guess what I  usually say you know so but also between  different news organizations in  dangerous places people really do bond  together and it's a great question about  solidarity because when you're out there  you you do you know in a way normally  and that especially in this town that  there's a lot of competition it in many  ways that disappears wouldn't you  all right one last question then we can  stay and talk well thank you I'm not  entirely sure how to ask this question  but I want to follow up on a couple of  points that you made you said white why  are we not paying more attention to  what's going on in Aleppo and you said  that we're really in Europe so much of  this conflict now that it just kind of  rolls off of us and and it really kind  of does and one of the reasons I was  really looking forward tonight I figured  collectively your experiences are  amazing and they are but you know for  those of us that are trying to keep up  there are so many different players and  there's so many different conflicts and  all and then the the the external  players Russia the United States here at  Saudi Arabia it all seems pretty  hopeless and and so I'm just curious I  mean you know you said the highs and the  highs and the lows and the lows you know  where is there hope for all of this to  to somehow rather get sorted out or what  is your sense of this it is it's  incredibly complicated and don't feel  ignorant because I mean I literally have  to and I've studied the Middle East for  25 years you know to sit down and draw  graphs and put markers and try to put  things on maps and identify who's  fighting who there's a thousand militias  fighting on the opposition on the Syrian  opposition 